## Overview

The Pacific Music Festival Sapporo (PMF) is an international educational music festival founded in 1990 by Leonard Bernstein, the renowned American conductor and composer of West Side Story. The name Pacific carries dual meanings of the Pacific Ocean and peace, reflecting Bernstein''s vision of contributing to world peace through music education. Each summer, the festival runs for roughly three weeks to a month in Sapporo, where selected young musicians from around the world receive instruction from top-tier faculty and present their progress in public concerts.

PMF is recognized as one of the three major educational music festivals in the world, along with the Tanglewood Music Festival in the United States and the Schleswig-Holstein Musik Festival in Germany. Since its inception, it has produced approximately 3,900 musicians from 79 countries and regions. The main venue is the Sapporo Concert Hall Kitara, renowned for its exceptional acoustics, while performances also occur at the Sapporo Art Park outdoor stage, various locations across Hokkaido, and Tokyo. The core educational program, the PMF Academy, offers an intensive short-term curriculum where participants refine their orchestral and chamber music skills while fostering international exchange.

## History and Origin

Leonard Bernstein, celebrated for his compositions and conducting, devoted his later years to the education of young musicians. In 1990, he traveled to Sapporo with the London Symphony Orchestra and founded the Pacific Music Festival. However, Bernstein passed away on October 14 of the same year, only three months after founding the festival, casting doubt on the festival''s continuation beyond its first edition.

On December 31, 1990, at a memorial concert for Bernstein in New York, the then-mayor of Sapporo, Takeshi Itagaki, declared that the city would carry on Bernstein''s legacy. This decision ensured the festival''s survival in Sapporo. Itagaki''s declaration marked a historic turning point that secured the continuation of PMF, laying the foundation for Bernstein''s ideals of peace through music education to be passed on to future generations.

Since then, PMF has grown into an annual international educational music festival. Bernstein''s educational philosophy, which emphasizes not only technical skill but also mutual understanding between young people from different cultures and backgrounds, has been preserved. From the 1990s through the 2000s, the number of participating countries and the scale of concerts expanded, and PMF established partnerships with major music festivals worldwide.

In June 2019 the official name was changed to Pacific Music Festival Sapporo, adding the name of the host city to the festival itself. Today, PMF has established itself as a leading educational music festival in Asia. The message of peace across the Pacific that Bernstein envisioned is renewed each summer by approximately 100 academy students. Through concerts and community outreach activities, they continue to demonstrate the power of music.

## Highlights

**PMF Academy**
The core educational program of PMF, the Academy runs for about one month each summer. Academy students receive direct instruction from world-class faculty, including string players from the Vienna Philharmonic, wind and percussion players from the Berlin Philharmonic, and principal players from major North American orchestras. This environment provides practical learning opportunities rarely available at ordinary music schools, enabling participants to achieve remarkable growth in a short period. Rehearsals and private lessons are centered at the Sapporo Concert Hall Kitara, whose superior acoustics enhance the learning experience.

**PMF Orchestra Concerts**
Selected academy members form the PMF Orchestra and perform full-scale concerts under the baton of the chief conductor. The repertoire ranges from classical to contemporary works, with concerts held at Kitara mainly on weekends during the festival period. These concerts serve as the culmination of the academy students'' efforts, demanding professional-level performance. Audiences witness the moment when young talents from around the world come together to create a unified ensemble.

**Picnic Concert**
Held at the outdoor stage of Sapporo Art Park, this open-air concert extends from midday to dusk. Audiences can lie on the grass, eat, and drink while freely enjoying the music. This format was designed to make classical music accessible to newcomers and families, including those who might feel intimidated by formal concert halls. Free entry and exit are permitted, and even babies are welcome, offering a unique PMF experience of relaxing in nature while immersed in music.

**PMF GALA Concert**
Held at Kitara toward the end of the festival period, this is a festive celebratory concert. The name GALA, derived from the French word for celebration, reflects the program''s extended length and variety, with multiple conductors and soloists performing a diverse selection of pieces. Many audience members dress formally, creating a celebratory atmosphere. This concert is the culmination of PMF, where academy students and faculty together celebrate the joy of music.

**Link-Up Concert**
Based on Link Up, the music education program for elementary school students developed by Carnegie Hall in New York, this program has been held since 2013. Sixth-graders from Sapporo municipal elementary schools, who have studied the recorder and songs in class beforehand, perform together with the PMF Orchestra, guided by video and a host. Members of the public are also welcome to watch the children take part. The program extends the educational mission of PMF into the Sapporo community and cultivates the soil in which future performers grow, and for many of the children it is their first encounter with a full orchestra at close range.

**Host City Orchestra Concert**
The Sapporo Symphony Orchestra, as the host city orchestra, performs alongside PMF faculty and academy students. Founded in 1961, the Sapporo Symphony is a professional orchestra that serves as a vital partner in PMF''s operations. This concert offers a rare opportunity to enjoy the fusion of international artists and the local orchestra, showcasing the high standard of Sapporo''s musical culture.

**Free and Community Outreach Concerts**
PMF organizes outreach concerts such as the Volunteer Concert (now called Minamina Concert), and sends musicians to hospitals, welfare facilities, and schools where people normally have limited access to live performances. These activities embody Bernstein''s ideal of music for everyone, demonstrating that PMF is not merely a music festival but also a platform for social contribution.

## Event Information and Access

- **Period**: Typically held for about 21 days from early July to late July. The latest schedule and availability should be confirmed on the official website.
- **Main Venue**: Sapporo Concert Hall Kitara (located in Nakajima Park, Chuo-ku, Sapporo). This hall is internationally acclaimed as a dedicated classical music venue.
- **Other Venues**: Sapporo Art Park (outdoor stage), various halls in Sapporo, locations across Hokkaido, and Suntory Hall in Tokyo.
- **Access (Kitara)**: About 7 minutes on foot from Exit 3 of Nakajima Koen Station on the Sapporo Municipal Subway Namboku Line, or about 7 minutes from Exit 1 of Horohirabashi Station. The hall is also about 4 minutes on foot from the Nakajima Koen-dori streetcar stop. Public transportation is recommended due to limited parking.
- **Official Website**: https://www.pmf.or.jp/ provides the latest program, ticket information, and concert schedules.
- **Organizers**: The festival is organized by the Pacific Music Festival Organizing Committee together with the City of Sapporo, and co-organized by the Sapporo Cultural Arts Foundation, which operates Sapporo Concert Hall Kitara.
- **Inquiries**: Contact the Pacific Music Festival Organizing Committee (phone 011-242-2211) or use the inquiry form on the official website.
- **Tickets**: Major concerts are ticketed, and some programs such as the Link-Up Concert are offered at modest prices. For selected concerts, Youth Wing seats are provided free of charge to audiences aged 25 and under, from elementary school students upward. Tickets are sold through the official website and major ticket agencies.

## Surrounding Area

Sapporo Concert Hall Kitara is located within Nakajima Park, a green oasis in the city center with ponds and walking paths. Visitors can enjoy a stroll in the park between concerts, exploring the Japanese garden, tea house, and the Hokkaido Museum of Literature. Many concertgoers take a walk through the park before or after performances to savor the musical experience.

Sapporo''s summer, with average temperatures around 20 degrees Celsius, is pleasant and ideal for sightseeing. Odori Park hosts seasonal events, and the Sapporo TV Tower offers panoramic views. The Tanukikoji Shopping Arcade and the Sapporo Central Wholesale Market are popular spots for enjoying local cuisine. Combining PMF concerts with a visit to the Sapporo Summer Festival street stalls, a local summer tradition, is a common delight.

Sapporo is renowned for its ramen culture, particularly miso ramen, as well as Genghis Khan (grilled lamb) and fresh seafood. For international participants and visitors, experiencing Hokkaido''s food alongside the music enriches the festival experience. During the festival period, restaurants around Kitara bustle with activity day and night.

## Related Information

- **World''s Three Major Educational Music Festivals**: PMF is recognized alongside the Tanglewood Music Festival (founded 1940, USA) and the Schleswig-Holstein Musik Festival (founded 1986, Germany) as a leading educational music festival. All three focus on nurturing young musicians with world-class faculty.
- **Leonard Bernstein**: Founder of PMF, legendary composer, and conductor. His educational philosophy remains the festival''s core.
- **Sapporo Symphony Orchestra**: Professional orchestra established in 1961, which in recent years has performed as PMF''s host city orchestra. It is a central pillar of Sapporo''s musical culture.
- **PMF Organizing Committee**: Officially the Pacific Music Festival Organizing Committee, a public interest incorporated foundation that organizes the festival together with the City of Sapporo to ensure the festival''s continuity and development.
- **Sapporo Concert Hall Kitara**: Opened in 1997 as a dedicated classical music venue, internationally praised for its acoustic design. It is the main venue for PMF.
- **Other Music Festivals in Sapporo**: The city hosts additional summer music events, such as the Sapporo International Music Festival, offering diverse genres across various venues.','pacific-music-festival','pacific-music-festival','2026-08-08 17:30:01','2026-05-20T15:07:52.470287+00:00','wikidata','2026-08-16 13:41:19','Ku-ma96','CC BY-SA 4.0','https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sapporo_Art_Park_Outdoor_Stage_%282017%29.jpg',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "festivals" VALUES('Q746798','節分','Setsubun','雑節の一つ、各季節の始まりの日の前日、及びその日に行われる行事','Japanese holiday',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,2,'winter','https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Setsubun.jpg','https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E7%AF%80%E5%88%86','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Setsubun',85,'drafted','## 概要

節分（せつぶん）は、日本全国で行われる季節の行事で、現在では一般に立春の前日（おおむね2月3日ごろ）を指します。「鬼は外、福は内」のかけ声とともに豆をまく「豆まき」がよく知られ、邪気を払い、一年の無病息災や幸福を願う年中行事として、家庭・寺社を問わず広く親しまれています。冬の最も寒い時期に行われながら、暦のうえでは春の到来を目前にした節目であり、季節が入れ替わる境目を強く意識させる行事でもあります。

特定の地域だけの祭りではなく、全国の家庭で行われるとともに、各地の寺社では大規模な節分行事が催されます。近年では、その年の縁起の良い方角（恵方）を向いて太巻き寿司を無言で食べる「恵方巻」の習慣も広まり、季節を感じる行事として定着しています。ひとつの祭礼というより、宮中の儀礼・寺社の神事・家庭の習俗・食文化が層をなして重なった、日本の年中行事の縮図といえる存在です。

## 歴史・由来

「節分」とは本来、季節の変わり目を意味する言葉で、立春・立夏・立秋・立冬それぞれの前日を指していました。つまり一年に四回あった言葉です。そのうち、冬から春への変わり目である立春の前日が、一年の始まりにあたる重要な節目として特に重んじられるようになり、今日では「節分」といえばこの日を指すのが一般的です。旧暦では立春が年の初めに近い位置にあったため、その前日は大晦日に相当する日と受け止められ、古い年の穢れを払って新しい年を迎えるという意識が強く働きました。

節分の豆まきの源流は、古代中国から伝わった「追儺（ついな）」という宮中行事にあるとされています。追儺は、疫病や災いの象徴である鬼を追い払う厄除けの儀式で、日本では文武天皇の時代、慶雲3年（706年）には行われていたことが『続日本紀』に記されていると伝えられます。この年に疫病が広く流行したことが背景にあったと伝えられ、目に見えない病の原因を鬼という形に置き換えて追い払おうとした、当時の人々の切実な願いがうかがえます。

宮中の追儺では、金色の四つの目を持つ仮面をつけた「方相氏（ほうそうし）」と呼ばれる役が矛と盾を手に現れ、疫鬼を追い立てる役割を担いました。ところが時代が下ると、この異形の方相氏自身が鬼と見なされ、追われる側に回るという逆転が起こったと伝えられます。宮中行事が民間へ広まる過程で、豆をまいて鬼を追い払う現在の形へと発展していったと考えられており、追う者と追われる者の入れ替わりは、儀礼が担い手を変えながら意味そのものを変えていったことを示す一例といえます。

豆をまくのは、「魔（ま）を滅（め）する」という語呂や、穀物に宿る力で邪気を払うという考えなどに由来するとされます。炒った豆を用いるのは、まいた豆から芽が出ると災いが再び根づくと考えられたためとも伝えられます。ただし起源や意味づけには諸説があり、確定した一つの説明があるわけではありません。ここでは主要な伝えに基づいて概観しています。

## 見どころ

**家庭の豆まき**
節分を象徴する行事が豆まきです。炒った大豆を「鬼は外、福は内」と唱えながらまき、まいた豆を年の数、または年の数に一つ加えた数だけ食べて、一年の健康を願います。地域によっては落花生を用いますが、これは雪の上や屋外でも拾いやすく衛生的だという実際的な理由からとされ、風習が土地の条件に合わせて姿を変えてきたことがうかがえます。家庭では、家族の誰かが鬼の面をつけて鬼役となり、子どもたちが豆をまくほほえましい光景も見られます。

**寺社の節分会と福豆まき**
各地の寺社では、大規模な節分会（せつぶんえ）が催されます。年男・年女や著名人が裃（かみしも）姿で登場し、参拝者に向けて福豆をまく福豆まきは、多くの人でにぎわう人気の行事です。年男・年女とはその年の干支に生まれた人を指し、めでたい年回りにある者が福を配るという考えに基づいています。豆をまく側に立つことが名誉とされてきた背景には、厄を払う力を託された役割という理解があります。

**吉田神社の追儺式と火炉祭**
京都市の吉田神社の節分祭は、古式に近い形を今に伝える行事として知られます。節分前日にあたる日の夕刻、本宮前の舞殿で追儺式（鬼やらい神事）が行われ、黄金の四つ目の仮面をかぶった方相氏が松明を持つ供を従えて登場し、疫鬼を追い払います。節分当日の深夜には、三ノ鳥居前に設けられた火炉で、納められた古い神札を焚き上げる火炉祭が営まれます。宮中儀礼の面影と、火を用いて古い年を送る民間の感覚とが一続きに並ぶ点に、この社の節分の特色があります。

**成田山新勝寺の福は内**
千葉県の成田山新勝寺の節分会は、かけ声に特徴があります。ここでは「鬼は外」を唱えず、「福は内」だけを繰り返します。御本尊である不動明王の御前では鬼さえも改心し、追い払う必要がないという理解に基づくものとされ、同じ節分でも寺社の信仰の内容によって作法が変わることを示しています。御護摩祈祷に続いて行われる開運豆まきには年男が参加し、追儺羽織をまとって豆をまきます。

**恵方巻**
その年の恵方を向き、太巻き寿司を切らずに丸ごと、無言で食べきるという習慣です。大正初めには大阪の限られた地域で行われていたとの証言があり、戦後には大阪の海苔問屋の同業組合による普及活動が行われました。1983年にコンビニエンスストアが大阪と兵庫の一部店舗で販売を始めたことをきっかけに商業的な広がりを見せ、他チェーンの追随を経て、2010年代には関西以外の地域にも定着しました。地域の習俗が流通網に乗って全国化した、比較的新しい伝播の事例として興味深い習慣です。

**柊鰯と鰯の食**
西日本を中心に、柊の枝に焼いた鰯の頭を刺した「柊鰯（ひいらぎいわし）」を戸口に飾る風習があります。柊の棘が鬼の目を刺し、鰯を焼く煙と匂いが鬼を寄せつけないという考えに基づくとされます。鰯を焼いて食べる家庭もあり、豆まきが音と動作で邪気を払うのに対し、こちらは棘と匂いという別の手段で同じ目的を果たそうとする点に、魔除けの発想の幅が表れています。

## 開催情報・アクセス

開催日は例年、立春の前日にあたります。多くの年は2月3日ですが、立春の日付は年によってわずかに動くため、2月2日や2月4日になる年もあります。日付が固定されていない点は、節分が暦の計算に基づく行事であることのあらわれです。

会場は全国に広がります。家庭ではそれぞれの家で豆まきを行い、寺社では節分会が営まれます。京都の吉田神社、千葉の成田山新勝寺のほか、各地の著名な寺社で大規模な行事が行われます。

行事の内容は寺社ごとに異なります。追儺の神事を古式にのっとって行うところ、著名人を招いた福豆まきを中心とするところ、火を用いた神事を組み合わせるところなど、同じ節分でも構成に大きな幅があります。

参加方法も一様ではありません。豆まきの担い手として参加するには事前の申し込みや志納が必要な場合があり、募集人数や条件は寺社によって異なります。参加を希望する場合は、各寺社の案内を早めに確認してください。

混雑への備えが要ります。人気の行事には多くの参拝者が集まり、周辺の道路や交通機関も混み合います。公共交通機関の利用と、時間に余裕を持った行動をおすすめします。

最新の情報は各寺社の公式発表で確認してください。開催日時・行事の内容・参加条件は年によって変わることがあり、実施の可否も含めて公式の案内が確実です。

## 周辺の見どころ

節分の行事は全国各地の寺社で行われるため、訪れる地域に応じてさまざまな楽しみ方ができます。著名な寺社の節分会に参加すれば、その土地の歴史や信仰にふれることができ、周辺の観光や食とあわせて季節の行事を満喫できます。京都であれば吉田神社の周辺に社寺が集まっており、成田であれば参道の店並みとあわせて歩くことができます。

立春前後は、梅の便りが聞かれ始める時期でもあり、地域によっては梅の名所めぐりも楽しめます。寒さの厳しい時期ではありますが、春の到来を予感させる季節の行事として、各地の冬の風情とあわせて味わうことができます。

食の面でも節分は楽しみの多い時期です。恵方巻を扱う店は当日に向けて品ぞろえを整え、鰯を出す料理店もあります。行事に参加したあとにその土地の節分の食を味わうことで、同じ節分という行事が地域ごとに違う形をとってきたことを実感できます。

## 関連情報

節分は、日本の代表的な年中行事のひとつであり、立春という暦の節目と結びついた厄除け・招福の行事です。

宮中の追儺に由来しながら、長い歴史のなかで民間の豆まきや寺社の節分会へと広がっていきました。

方相氏が追う側から追われる側へ転じたと伝えられるように、儀礼の意味は時代とともに変化してきました。

恵方巻のように、比較的新しく全国へ広まった習慣も、今日では節分の一部となっています。

柊鰯や落花生の使用など地域ごとの差異も大きく、ひとつの型に収まらない行事です。

なお、起源や意味づけ、豆まきの作法、恵方巻などの習慣には地域差や諸説があり、本記事は全国的に見られる一般的な特徴を概観したものです。具体的な日付や各寺社の行事内容は年や場所によって異なるため、参加の際は最新の情報をご確認ください。
','## Overview

Setsubun is a seasonal observance held throughout Japan, today generally referring to the day before Risshun, the first day of spring, which falls around February 3. It is well known for mamemaki, or bean-throwing, in which beans are scattered to the call of Oni wa soto, fuku wa uchi, meaning demons out, fortune in, and it is widely cherished as an annual event in homes, temples, and shrines alike, a day for driving away evil and praying for a year of good health and happiness. Although it falls in the coldest part of winter, on the calendar it stands immediately before the arrival of spring, and it is an observance that makes people keenly aware of the boundary where one season gives way to another.

Rather than a festival belonging to one particular region, it is carried out in homes across the country, while temples and shrines in many places hold large-scale Setsubun events. In recent years the custom of ehomaki, silently eating a thick sushi roll while facing the lucky direction of the year, has also spread, and it has become established as an event by which people feel the turning of the season. More than a single rite, Setsubun is a layered accumulation of court ceremony, shrine and temple ritual, household custom, and food culture, and it can be read as a miniature of the Japanese ceremonial year as a whole.

## History and Origins

The word setsubun originally meant the turning point of a season, referring to the day before each of Risshun, Rikka, Risshu, and Ritto, the first days of spring, summer, autumn, and winter. In other words, it was a term that occurred four times in a single year. Of these, the day before Risshun, the turning point from winter to spring, came to be especially valued as an important juncture marking the start of the year, and today setsubun generally refers to this day alone. Because Risshun stood close to the beginning of the year in the old lunisolar calendar, the day before it was received as an equivalent of New Year''s Eve, and a strong sense developed of sweeping away the defilement of the old year before greeting the new one.

The source of Setsubun''s bean-throwing is said to lie in a court observance called tsuina, transmitted from ancient China. Tsuina was a rite for warding off misfortune by driving away the demons that symbolized epidemics and calamities, and in Japan it is said to be recorded in the Shoku Nihongi as having been performed by the time of Emperor Monmu, in 706, the third year of the Keiun era. An epidemic is said to have spread widely in that year, and behind the rite one can sense the urgent wish of people who gave the invisible cause of disease the visible form of a demon so that it could be driven out.

In the court tsuina, a functionary known as the hososhi appeared wearing a mask with four golden eyes and carrying a halberd and shield, taking the role of the one who drove the plague demons away. As the centuries passed, however, it is said that a reversal occurred in which this strange figure was itself regarded as a demon and came to be the one pursued. As the court observance spread among ordinary people, it is thought to have developed into the present form in which beans are scattered to drive away demons, and the exchange of roles between pursuer and pursued stands as one example of how a ritual can change its very meaning as it changes hands.

The throwing of beans is said to derive from wordplay meaning to destroy evil, and from the idea of warding off harm with the power that dwells in grain. Roasted beans are used, according to one account, because a sprouting bean would mean that misfortune had taken root once again. There are, however, various theories about the origins and the meanings attached to them, and no single settled explanation exists. What follows is an overview based on the principal traditions.

## Highlights

**Bean-Throwing in the Home**
The observance that symbolizes Setsubun is bean-throwing. Roasted soybeans are scattered while chanting Oni wa soto, fuku wa uchi, and people eat the scattered beans in a number equal to their age, or their age plus one, praying for a year of health. Some regions use peanuts instead, a choice attributed to the practical reason that they are easy to pick up and remain clean even on snow or outdoors, which shows how a custom reshapes itself to suit local conditions. In homes one can also see the charming sight of a family member donning a demon mask to play the demon while the children throw the beans.

**Setsubun Rites and Lucky-Bean Throwing at Temples and Shrines**
At temples and shrines in many places, large-scale Setsubun rites known as setsubun-e are held. The lucky-bean throwing, in which the men and women of the year''s zodiac sign and well-known figures appear in formal kamishimo attire and throw beans toward worshippers, is a popular event that draws large crowds. The men and women of the year are those born under the same zodiac sign as the current year, and the practice rests on the idea that people standing in an auspicious year should be the ones to distribute good fortune. Behind the honor traditionally attached to being among the throwers lies the understanding that they have been entrusted with the power to dispel misfortune.

**The Tsuina Rite and Karo-sai of Yoshida Shrine**
The Setsubun festival of Yoshida Shrine in Kyoto is known for preserving a form close to the ancient rite. On the evening of the day before Setsubun, the tsuina rite, popularly called oniyarai, is performed at the dance hall before the main sanctuary, where the hososhi appears in a mask with four golden eyes, accompanied by attendants bearing torches, and drives the plague demons away. Late on the night of Setsubun itself, the karo-sai is conducted at a great fire pit set before the third torii gate, where old talismans that have been returned to the shrine are burned. The distinctive character of this shrine''s observance lies in the way the vestige of court ceremony and the popular sense of sending off the old year by fire stand side by side in a single sequence.

**Fuku wa Uchi Alone at Naritasan Shinshoji**
The Setsubun rite at Naritasan Shinshoji in Chiba Prefecture is distinguished by its chant. Here the phrase demons out is not spoken at all, and only fortune in is repeated. This is said to rest on the understanding that even demons undergo a change of heart in the presence of the principal image, Fudo Myoo, so that there is no need to drive them away, and it shows how the manner of observance can differ according to the content of faith at each institution even within the same festival. In the fortune-inviting bean-throwing that follows the goma fire ritual, the men of the year take part, wearing tsuina robes as they throw.

**Ehomaki**
This is the custom of facing the lucky direction of the year and eating a thick sushi roll whole and uncut, in silence, from beginning to end. Accounts indicate that the custom existed in limited areas of Osaka by the early Taisho period, and after the war a trade association of Osaka seaweed wholesalers carried out activities to promote it. Commercial expansion followed after a convenience store chain began selling the rolls at some outlets in Osaka and Hyogo in 1983, and as other chains followed suit the custom took hold beyond the Kansai region during the 2010s. It is an interesting case of a local custom carried to nationwide currency by distribution networks, and thus of relatively recent transmission.

**Hiiragi-Iwashi and Sardines**
Chiefly in western Japan there is a custom of displaying at the doorway a hiiragi-iwashi, the grilled head of a sardine skewered on a branch of hiiragi (false holly, Osmanthus heterophyllus). It is said to rest on the idea that the spiny leaves of hiiragi pierce the demon''s eyes while the smoke and smell of the grilling sardine keep the demon from approaching. Some households also grill and eat the sardines themselves, and where bean-throwing repels evil through sound and motion, this practice pursues the same end through thorn and odor, showing the breadth of thinking behind protective charms.

## Event Information and Access

The date each year is the day before Risshun. In most years this is February 3, but because the date of Risshun shifts slightly from year to year, there are years in which Setsubun falls on February 2 or February 4. That the date is not fixed is itself an expression of the fact that Setsubun is governed by calendrical calculation.

The venues extend across the whole country. In homes each household performs its own bean-throwing, while temples and shrines hold setsubun-e. Alongside Yoshida Shrine in Kyoto and Naritasan Shinshoji in Chiba, large-scale events take place at renowned temples and shrines in many regions.

The content of the observance differs by institution. Some perform the tsuina rite according to ancient forms, some center on lucky-bean throwing with invited celebrities, and some combine rites that use fire, so that the structure varies considerably even within the same festival.

Methods of participation are not uniform either. Taking part as one of the throwers may require advance application and an offering, and the number of places and the conditions differ by institution. Those who wish to participate should check the guidance of each temple or shrine well in advance.

Preparation for crowds is necessary. Popular events gather large numbers of worshippers, and the surrounding roads and transport services become congested. Using public transportation and allowing ample time are recommended.

For the latest details, please consult the official announcements of each temple or shrine. Dates, times, event content, and conditions of participation can change from year to year, and official guidance is the reliable source, including on whether an event will be held at all.

## Nearby Attractions

Because Setsubun observances take place at temples and shrines all over the country, visitors can enjoy them in various ways depending on the region. Attending the setsubun-e of a renowned institution offers contact with the history and faith of that locality, and the seasonal event can be enjoyed together with nearby sightseeing and food. In Kyoto, shrines and temples cluster around Yoshida Shrine, while in Narita the approach to the temple can be walked alongside its long line of shops.

The period around Risshun is also when the first news of plum blossoms begins to be heard, and in some regions one can enjoy touring famous plum-viewing spots. Although the cold is at its most severe, the observance can be savored together with the winter character of each region as a seasonal event that hints at the coming of spring.

Food is another pleasure of the season. Shops selling ehomaki prepare their offerings for the day, and some restaurants serve sardines. Tasting the local foods of Setsubun after attending an event brings home the realization that a single observance has taken different forms from place to place.

## Related Information

Setsubun is one of Japan''s representative annual observances, a rite of warding off misfortune and inviting good fortune that is bound to the calendrical juncture of Risshun.

While derived from the court tsuina, over its long history it spread outward into the bean-throwing of ordinary households and the setsubun-e of temples and shrines.

As the tradition that the hososhi turned from pursuer into pursued suggests, the meaning of the ritual has shifted with the passing of the ages.

Customs of relatively recent nationwide spread, such as ehomaki, have also become part of Setsubun today.

Regional differences are considerable, from hiiragi-iwashi to the use of peanuts, and the observance does not fit into any single mold.

Please note that there are regional variations and competing theories concerning the origins, the meanings attached to them, the manner of bean-throwing, and customs such as ehomaki, and that this article offers an overview of general features seen nationwide. Because the specific date and the content of each institution''s events differ by year and place, please check the latest information before attending.
