## Overview

The Hirakata Large Chrysanthemum Doll Festival (Hirakata O-kiku Ningyo) was a traditional exhibition held in Hirakata City, Osaka Prefecture, for approximately 100 years, featuring dolls dressed in garments made of chrysanthemum flowers. A chrysanthemum doll (kiku ningyo) is an artistic work that uses small chrysanthemums with flexible stems (ningyo-giku) attached to the doll''s body to recreate scenes from historical tales or theatrical performances. It has been acclaimed as a comprehensive art form that integrates the skills of specialized craftspeople, including doll makers, chrysanthemum specialists, cultivation experts, set designers, art decorators, and lighting technicians .

This event originated from a chrysanthemum doll exhibition started by Keihan Electric Railway in 1910 at Kori-en, and from the third edition in 1912, it began to be held in Hirakata . Although it continued as the beloved Hirakata Large Chrysanthemum Doll Festival through interruptions due to war and changes in venue, it closed its annual history in 2005 due to the aging of craftspeople, a shortage of successors, and declining visitor numbers . Today, the Hirakata Citizens'' Chrysanthemum Doll Association, a volunteer group formed to inherit this tradition, continues to produce and display chrysanthemum dolls on a smaller scale.

## History and Origin

The origin of the Hirakata Large Chrysanthemum Doll Festival dates back to a chrysanthemum doll exhibition titled Setsugetsuka (Snow, Moon, and Flowers) held by Keihan Electric Railway in 1910 at Kori-en (now in Neyagawa City) . The following year, in 1911, an exhibition themed Chushingura (The Loyal 47 Ronin) was held, laying the foundation for later chrysanthemum doll displays in Hirakata. Initially operated by Gifu Kikuraku-en, the first chrysanthemum doll exhibition in Hirakata was held from October 6 to November 25, 1912, on a site of approximately 10,000 square meters near Hirakata Station (now Hirakata-koen Station). The scale at that time was about three times larger than the Kori-en era, and the event developed steadily thereafter .

From the late Taisho to early Showa periods, the content of the chrysanthemum doll displays became increasingly enriched. In 1915, chrysanthemum craftspeople, cultivation specialists, and painters joined the production team, establishing a highly specialized workforce . During the Showa period, themes based on films, kabuki plays, and historical novels were adopted, with different stories recreated each year. Examples include the 1927 theme Kana-tehon Chushingura (Film Adaptation), the 1929 theme Taikoki (The Tale of Hideyoshi), and the 1930 theme Chushingura, reflecting the popular trends of each era .

The exhibition was suspended during World War II but resumed in Senriyama in 1946, and returned to Hirakata on October 1, 1949, after a six-year hiatus . For this revival, in addition to the traditional dan-gaeshi (quick scene changes in darkness), a revue-style format featuring dancers was introduced with direction from the Takarazuka Revue, adding new appeal . In 1950, the Chrysanthemum Art Association was established, aiming for chrysanthemum art, and a library building for collecting and displaying chrysanthemum-related materials was constructed .

In 1953, a large theater hall capable of accommodating 4,000 people was completed, further expanding the scale of the chrysanthemum doll exhibition . From this year, the event was sponsored by the Asahi Shimbun newspaper and became an established autumn tradition. Although it continued into the Heisei period, the 96-year history ended with the 2005 theme Yoshitsune . The reasons for closure were the aging of craftspeople, a shortage of successors, and declining visitor numbers. To prevent the tradition from dying out, the Hirakata Citizens'' Chrysanthemum Doll Association was established in April 2006 by citizen volunteers .

## Highlights

**Cultivation and Preservation of Ningyo-Giku Chrysanthemums**
The Hirakata Citizens'' Chrysanthemum Doll Association cultivates and preserves approximately 30 varieties of ningyo-giku chrysanthemums, in fields at Nakaburi in Hirakata City, while parent stock is kept at Supple Murano . Ningyo-giku is a general term for small chrysanthemums with flexible stems suitable for doll costumes, requiring special cultivation techniques. This preservation activity is crucial for maintaining the chrysanthemum varieties used in the former Hirakata Large Chrysanthemum Doll Festival, and the flowers carefully grown in local fields are used for autumn displays. The sight of colorful chrysanthemums blooming across the fields conveys the weight of tradition and the passion of the citizens .

**The Chrysanthemum Attachment Process and Craftsmanship**
The most impressive part of the production process is kiku-zuke, the attaching of the flowers. Around October, as the exhibition approaches, the chrysanthemums grown through the year are fixed to the body of the doll. Each plant has its roots wrapped in sphagnum moss, is bent into a U shape and inserted into a torso frame built from bamboo and straw, and is then tied in place at a position that tradition has fixed in advance. The number of blooms varies with the costume, and layered garments such as the juni-hitoe require far more flowers than an ordinary figure. The work demands skill and concentration alike, and the sureness with which each soft stem is bent without breaking is what visitors remember most.

**Bamboo and Straw Body Frames**
The do-gara (body frame), which forms the skeleton of the chrysanthemum doll, is handmade from bamboo and straw. This traditional construction method has been inherited from the former Hirakata Large Chrysanthemum Doll Festival and is a crucial process that determines the doll''s pose and costume silhouette . The work of splitting bamboo and wrapping straw to shape the doll requires extensive experience and many steps. By attaching chrysanthemums to this frame, the soft flowers cover the hard skeleton, creating a lifelike work of art.

**The Dan-Gaeshi Scene Change Technique**
The dan-gaeshi (quick scene changes in darkness), a famous feature since before the war, involved dimming the lights and instantly exchanging dolls and backgrounds to change scenes . This technique amazed and delighted audiences and was one of the main highlights of the chrysanthemum doll exhibition. After the postwar revival, direction from the Takarazuka Revue was incorporated, adding a revue-style format with dancers, making the stage even more spectacular.

**Citizen Volunteer Production Activities**
The Hirakata Citizens'' Chrysanthemum Doll Association began its work with ten citizen volunteers under the guidance of the Takekawa doll master, who had been active in the former Hirakata Dai Kiku-Ningyo, and today its members range from beginners to experienced hands. They carry out every process themselves rather than dividing the labour among specialists: taking cuttings of ningyo-giku, splitting bamboo, making thin strips and straw bundles, forming heads and torso frames, repairing props and attaching the chrysanthemums. The association meets four days a week, on Mondays, Tuesdays, Thursdays and Fridays, and that steady rhythm is what allows a craft once sustained by professional artisans to survive as community culture.

**Display Locations and Free Admission**
The current Citizens'' Chrysanthemum Doll Exhibition is held at various locations in the city, including the north side of the Hirakata City Hall Annex and the second-floor concourse of Hirakata-shi Station, all with free admission . Unlike the former large-scale paid exhibitions, this format allows citizens to easily enjoy the displays. During the exhibition period, the dolls'' costumes are changed between the first and second halves to match the chrysanthemum blooming conditions, allowing visitors to enjoy different expressions on each visit.

## Event Information and Access

1. **Event name**: Hirakata Dai Kiku-Ningyo, the large-scale chrysanthemum doll exhibition that closed in 2005. What is held today is the Citizens'' Chrysanthemum Doll Exhibition, a separate community undertaking presented as part of the Hirakata Chrysanthemum Festival.
2. **Period**: Typically from early October to late November (check the official website for the latest schedule and availability)
3. **Venues**: North side of Hirakata City Hall Annex, second-floor concourse of Hirakata-shi Station, Shimin no Mori (Citizens'' Forest), special stage at Nippe Park Okadai Chuo, among others
4. **Admission**: Free
5. **Organizer**: Hirakata Citizens'' Chrysanthemum Doll Association
6. **Contact**: Hirakata Citizens'' Chrysanthemum Doll Association (Phone: 072-840-7869)
7. **Access**: Within walking distance from Hirakata-shi Station on the Keihan Electric Railway (varies by venue)
8. **Production Activity Visits**: The activities of the Hirakata Citizens'' Chrysanthemum Doll Association can be observed at Sapuri Murano (5-1 Murano-nishimachi, Hirakata City) on activity days (Monday, Tuesday, Thursday, Friday, 10:00 AM to approximately 4:00 PM, excluding holidays)

## Surrounding Area Information

Hirakata City developed as a town centered around the major Keihan Electric Railway stations, and the area around Hirakata Park (near Hirakata-koen Station), which served as the venue for the Hirakata Large Chrysanthemum Doll Festival, remains a popular recreation spot for citizens. Hirakata Park is a historic amusement park opened in 1912, supporting Hirakata''s tourism alongside the chrysanthemum dolls . Today, the park features attractions such as a Ferris wheel and roller coasters, bustling with families.

In addition, the area around Hirakata-shi Station has a concentration of commercial facilities and restaurants, allowing visitors to enjoy shopping and dining before or after viewing the chrysanthemum dolls. The second-floor concourse of Hirakata-shi Station is one of the main venues for the Citizens'' Chrysanthemum Doll Exhibition, providing an environment where station users can easily appreciate the artistic works . Furthermore, the venue on the north side of the City Hall Annex is located in the city center, offering display spaces integrated with public facilities.

Hirakata City is also situated along the historic Kyokaido Road, once thriving as a post town. During the chrysanthemum doll season, chrysanthemums cultivated by citizens are also displayed along the Kyokaido Road, enhancing the autumn atmosphere . By exploring these surrounding spots, visitors can gain a deeper experience of Hirakata''s culture and history.

## Related Information

1. **Materials of the Takekawa doll master**: The doll heads, preliminary sketches and drawings made by the Takekawa doll master, who was active in the former Hirakata Dai Kiku-Ningyo, have been inherited by the Hirakata Citizens'' Chrysanthemum Doll Association. They form a valuable record of how the heads were modelled and painted, and they allow the association to trace techniques that would otherwise have survived only in the memory of those who practised them.
2. **Hirakata Chrysanthemum Festival**: The Hirakata Chrysanthemum Festival is the collective name for three events centred on the chrysanthemum, the city flower: the Hirakata Chrysanthemum Show, the Citizens'' Chrysanthemum Doll Exhibition and the Hirakata-juku Kaido Chrysanthemum Festival. The doll exhibition is held as one of the three, featuring chrysanthemum-related events at various locations in the city . This festival collaborates with citizens'' chrysanthemum cultivation activities, creating a comprehensive event that colors Hirakata''s autumn.
3. **Volunteer Membership Recruitment**: The Hirakata Citizens'' Chrysanthemum Doll Association continuously recruits volunteer members who can experience the techniques of chrysanthemum doll making . Beginners are welcome, and participants can learn traditional techniques while contributing to their preservation.
4. **Variety Preservation Efforts**: The association preserves and cultivates approximately 30 varieties of ningyo-giku, including some inherited from the Hirakata Large Chrysanthemum Doll Festival era . This activity is also important for maintaining the genetic diversity of chrysanthemums.
5. **Revival Exhibitions at Hirakata Park**: The Large Chrysanthemum Doll Festival was revived for limited periods in 2010 to commemorate the 100th anniversary of the Keihan Electric Railway''s opening, and in 2012 to commemorate the 100th anniversary of Hirakata Park''s opening . These revivals introduced topiary chrysanthemum dolls using a technique of creating body frames with gardening supports and wire, and growing potted chrysanthemums along them, in addition to traditional methods.
6. **Comparison with Similar Events**: While chrysanthemum dolls can be seen in other regions such as Gifu Prefecture, the Hirakata chrysanthemum dolls are distinctive for their unique evolution, including the dan-gaeshi technique and the incorporation of revue-style formats . In particular, the inheritance activities by citizen volunteers are noteworthy as a model for preserving traditional culture.','hirakata-dai-kiku-ningyo','hirakata-dai-kiku-ningyo','2026-08-08 17:30:01','2026-05-20T15:07:52.470287+00:00','wikidata','2026-08-16 13:41:19','Jnn。','CC BY-SA 3.0','https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hirakata-kikuningyo3213.JPG',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "festivals" VALUES('Q11258475','あやべ水無月まつり','Ayabe Minazuki Festival',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'京都府',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%81%82%E3%82%84%E3%81%B9%E6%B0%B4%E7%84%A1%E6%9C%88%E3%81%BE%E3%81%A4%E3%82%8A',NULL,60,'pending',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-05-20T15:07:52.470287+00:00','wikidata','2026-08-11 10:43:07',NULL,NULL,NULL,'毎年7月第4土曜日','{"type": "nth_dow", "month": 7, "nth": 4, "dow": 5, "raw": "7月第4土曜日", "ctx": "あやべ水無月まつり（あやべみなつきまつり）とは、京都府綾部市で毎年7月第4土曜日に開催されている祭。あやべ水無月まつり実行委員会（綾部商工会議所内）の主催。 キ", "pos": 33}','あやべ水無月まつり（あやべみなつきまつり）とは、京都府綾部市で毎年7月第4土曜日に開催されている祭。あやべ水無月まつり実行委員会（綾部商工会議所内）の主催。 キ','rule_only','ok','');
